CARDS = [
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "In qbank you use BullMQ on Redis. Explain what that is doing at the operating system level.", "A worker process blocks on a Redis list — which means it is sitting in a read on a socket, in S state, consuming zero CPU, with its descriptor registered in epoll. When a job arrives, the packet interrupts the machine, the softirq walks it up TCP into the socket buffer, epoll marks the descriptor ready, libuv wakes and calls back into my handler. The reason the queue has to be in Redis rather than in my process is that separate Node processes have separate address spaces — there is no shared memory between them, so shared state has to live somewhere both can reach over a socket."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "Why did you need Redis in qbank rather than an in-process structure?", "Because the moment there is more than one process — cluster workers, or replicas in a deployment — nothing in-process is shared. Separate address spaces are the definition of a process. So the rate limiter counter, the job queue and anything cached have to live in a process everyone can reach. That is one problem, not four, and Redis is the usual answer to it."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "Your Fastify service runs in a container. Walk me through what a request actually touches.", "The NIC DMAs the packet into memory and raises an interrupt; a softirq walks it through netfilter — where the Service IP was rewritten to my pod's IP — and up TCP into a socket buffer; that marks my descriptor ready in epoll; my process, which was sleeping in <code>epoll_wait</code> at zero CPU, is made runnable and scheduled subject to its cgroup's CPU weight; libuv's poll phase returns, it calls back, reads into a Buffer that lives outside the V8 heap, the HTTP parser runs, and V8 invokes my handler. When I await Postgres, the function suspends and the loop serves other connections."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "How would you size memory for your Node service in Kubernetes?", "Start from the fact that the kernel kills on RSS, and RSS is the V8 heap plus Buffers plus the node binary and libraries — 50 to 100 MB — plus JIT code space, thread stacks and allocator fragmentation. So I set <code>--max-old-space-size</code> to about 60 to 75 percent of the container limit, so V8 hits its own limit first and throws a catchable error with a stack trace and optionally a heap snapshot, rather than getting an uncatchable SIGKILL that tells me nothing. Then I measure and adjust, and if RSS is unexplained I try MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "What would you change about a Dockerfile you inherited?", "Four things, in order of impact. Exec-form entrypoint straight to node rather than <code>npm start</code>, so the process is PID 1 and actually receives SIGTERM. A non-root USER, and drop capabilities in the security context. Multi-stage build so the compiler and dev dependencies do not ship. And instruction ordering so dependency installation sits above the source copy, because a layer's cache invalidation cascades to everything after it."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "How would you add graceful shutdown to a service that does not have it?", "Handle SIGTERM. Fail the readiness probe first and keep serving for a few seconds, because Kubernetes removes the endpoint and sends the signal concurrently and the removal takes time to propagate — closing immediately is what produces 502s on every deploy. Then <code>server.close()</code> plus <code>closeIdleConnections()</code>, drain the queue workers and the database pool, and set a hard exit deadline below <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> so I always exit on my own terms."),
("self_explanation", "Explaining your own work", "What metrics would you add to a Node service you were made responsible for?", "Event loop delay percentiles and event loop utilisation from <code>perf_hooks</code> — the first tells me whether something is blocking the loop, the second how close to saturated the process is, and it is a far better autoscaling signal than CPU. Then cgroup <code>nr_throttled</code>, read from <code>/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat</code>, because Node cannot tell me that and throttling is invisible in a CPU graph. Then pool acquisition wait time, because that queue is inside my process and no APM shows it by default."),
("config", "Configuration", "Which Node flags and environment variables actually matter in production, and why?", "<code>--max-old-space-size</code>, set below the container limit so V8 fails before the kernel does. <code>UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE</code>, only if file, DNS, crypto or zlib work is queueing. <code>MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2</code> when RSS is unexplained on a many-core machine. <code>NODE_ENV=production</code>, which frameworks branch on. And <code>--heapsnapshot-near-heap-limit</code> so a heap-limit death leaves you something to look at."),
("config", "Configuration", "Which kernel sysctls would you check on a machine serving a lot of connections?", "<code>net.core.somaxconn</code> and the application's listen backlog, since a full accept queue silently drops connections; <code>fs.file-max</code> and the process's <code>ulimit -n</code>; <code>net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range</code> and <code>tcp_tw_reuse</code> if the machine makes many outbound connections; <code>nf_conntrack_max</code> if it does NAT; and <code>vm.overcommit_memory</code> if it runs Redis. I would not touch <code>tcp_tw_recycle</code> — it was removed in 4.12 because it broke behind NAT."),
("config", "Configuration", "What does a hardened Kubernetes securityContext look like and why each field?", "<code>runAsNonRoot</code> with an explicit <code>runAsUser</code>, so a container escape is not host root. <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, which sets no_new_privs so the kernel ignores setuid bits at execve. <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> with an emptyDir where writes are genuinely needed. <code>capabilities: drop [ALL]</code>, adding back only a specific one if required. And <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>, which filters the syscall table. Every field is one kernel mechanism turned off."),
("config", "Configuration", "How do you decide the size of a database connection pool?", "By the database's capacity, not by your concurrency. Postgres is process-per-connection, so a few hundred backends is a heavy load on the machine — the pool exists to keep that number small, and making it bigger to fix latency usually makes things worse. Size it against <code>max_connections</code> across all your replicas, add pgbouncer if you have many instances, and instrument acquisition wait time so you can see the queue that lives in your process."),
("occasional", "Occasional", "What is NUMA and when does it matter to you?", "On a multi-socket machine each socket has memory that is fast for it and slower for the other, so a process whose threads and memory end up on different sockets can silently lose a large fraction of its memory performance. It matters for large database instances and anything memory-bandwidth-bound on big cloud instances; <code>numactl</code> and CPU affinity are the controls. On a small instance it is not your problem."),
("occasional", "Occasional", "What are huge pages and who wants them?", "2 MB or 1 GB pages instead of 4 KB, so one TLB entry covers 512 times as much memory. Databases with large buffer pools want them, because a big randomly-accessed working set otherwise misses the TLB constantly. But <em>transparent</em> huge pages defragment in the background and cause latency spikes, which is why MongoDB and Redis both tell you to disable THP while Postgres wants explicit huge pages."),
("occasional", "Occasional", "What is the vDSO?", "A small page of kernel-maintained code and data mapped read-only into every process, so calls like <code>clock_gettime</code> are answered entirely in user mode — about 20 nanoseconds and no system call. It is why <code>Date.now()</code> shows no syscall in strace, and why time reading is dramatically slower on VMs whose clock source is not the TSC, because the fast path is then unavailable."),
("occasional", "Occasional", "What is seccomp and would you enable it?", "A filter that inspects each system call's number and arguments and decides allow, deny or kill. Docker applies a default profile blocking about 44 of the roughly 350 calls, and Kubernetes exposes it as <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>. Yes, I would enable it — a Node server needs a small syscall set and an exploit needs a larger one, so it is one of the cheapest genuine security improvements available."),
("edge_case", "Edge case", "Why does console.log behave differently in a terminal and in Docker?", "Node makes stdout synchronous when it is a file or a TTY and asynchronous when it is a pipe, because a pipe can block indefinitely on a slow reader. In a container stdout is a pipe to the daemon, so writes are buffered — which means output can be lost on an abrupt exit, and if the log consumer stalls the pipe fills and your application blocks on <code>console.log</code>. In a terminal it is a synchronous syscall per call, which is measurable in a hot path."),
("edge_case", "Edge case", "Why does os.cpus().length report 64 in a container limited to one core?", "<code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> is not namespaced, so it reports the host's cores. CPU limits live in cgroup files, which is a different place entirely. This is why the JVM needed explicit container support and why Node added <code>os.availableParallelism()</code> — a library sizing a pool from <code>cpus().length</code> creates 64 threads to fight over 100 milliseconds of quota."),
("edge_case", "Edge case", "Why can't you catch a segmentation fault in a native addon from JavaScript?", "It is a hardware exception — the memory management unit refused an access — delivered as SIGSEGV below the level at which V8 has any say. By the time the signal arrives there is no JavaScript stack to unwind, so <code>uncaughtException</code> cannot help. It is a genuine argument for running risky native work in a separate process with a supervisor."),
("edge_case", "Edge case", "Why does an unused setInterval keep a Node process alive?", "Because the loop exits when nothing at all is pending — there is no notion of main having finished, only of no work remaining. A live timer is pending work, as is an unclosed server or socket. <code>unref()</code> marks a handle as not keeping the loop alive, which is exactly what it is for."),
("edge_case", "Edge case", "Why is process.nextTick more dangerous than setImmediate?", "The nextTick queue is drained completely after every callback, including anything added while draining — so a nextTick that schedules another loops forever and the event loop never advances. No I/O, no timers, the process apparently hung at 100% CPU. setImmediate cannot do that, because it lands in the check phase of the <em>next</em> iteration, so the loop always gets a turn."),
]
